{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785003508,"nonce":"LE2bMkK5wHkfB6+OlddaDdqV9+A=","title":"**Deferred from 70aa42e** (PR #81), which fixed the runbook half only.","message":"A GitHub App can be misconfigured in two ways that both **fail silently**: an empty webhook-event subscription (Scarab still receives `installation`/`installation_repositories`, so `/v1/repos` looks healthy while no run ever triggers), and a missing `statuses:write` grant (every status post 403s while the run itself goes green). Today the only defence is documentation — the operator finds out by noticing nothing happened.\n\n**What to build:** a preflight/doctor endpoint that mints an installation token and diffs the App's *actual* granted permissions and subscribed events against what Scarab needs, reporting the gap. Natural home is the connections surface added by ADR-0060 (`GET /v1/connections` already reports `credential_present`; this is the same idea taken one level deeper), so a misconfigured App shows as unhealthy in Settings rather than as silence.\n\n**Acceptance sketch**\n- [ ] An endpoint reports, per GitHub connection, which required events/permissions are missing.\n- [ ] Settings surfaces it next to the existing credential health line.\n- [ ] Fake-forge test for both failure shapes (no events subscribed; statuses grant absent).\n- [ ] Never returns credential material.","files":null}]}